<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/expert/near-native-fluency">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Near-native fluency is <strong>the expert tier: precision, register control, humour and
    cultural navigation</strong>. The plateau that stops most learners is where the expert game
    begins — progress becomes targeted (fossilised errors, prosody, cultural reference) rather
    than general, and the metrics shift from volume to quality.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="plateau, fossil surgery, accent choice, cultural depth" widget="equationStep" config={nearNativeCfg} />

  <h2>The maintenance habit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use it or lose it (partially):</strong> attrition affects low-frequency vocabulary and speed first — maintenance input (media, conversation) keeps the system warm at a fraction of the learning cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Live the language, don't study it:</strong> at this tier, consuming native media for ENJOYMENT is the practice — the content is the curriculum. Language becomes a lens, not a subject.</li>
    <li><strong>Professional certification:</strong> C2 exams, interpreter certification (the translation lesson), or university-level study formalise the tier — for those who need the paper.</li>
    <li><strong>The identity layer:</strong> near-native speakers often report dreaming and joking in the language — the point where the language stops being foreign. The multilingual-cognition lesson asks what that does to the mind.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> CEFR C2 descriptors; Selinker & Lakshmanan on fossilization; actors' dialect-coaching literature.</p>
</TopicPage>
